# 101. Symmetric Tree

[LeetCode - Symmetric Tree](https://leetcode.com/problems/symmetric-tree/)

## My Approach

At first, I thought about using level order traversal with a queue, since symmetry seems related to comparing nodes level by level.

But while thinking about how to actually compare the nodes, the queue approach started getting messy.

Then I noticed something simpler:

The root divides the tree into two separate subtrees.

So instead of asking:

"Is this whole tree symmetric?"

I can ask:

"Are the left and right subtrees mirrors of each other?"

That makes the problem much more natural for recursion.

---

## The Mirror Observation

Suppose we have two subtrees.

For them to be mirrors:

- Left side of the first tree must match the right side of the second tree.
- Right side of the first tree must match the left side of the second tree.

So:

    root2.left  <->  root3.right
    root2.right <->  root3.left

Every recursive call therefore compares opposite branches.

---

## The Three Cases

When comparing two nodes, there are three important cases.

### 1. Both are null

    root2 = null
    root3 = null

Both sides ended at the same time.

Therefore, this part is symmetric.

    return true;

### 2. Only one is null

    root2 = null
    root3 = node

or the opposite.

One tree has a node where the other does not.

Therefore, their structures are different.

    return false;

### 3. Values are different

If both nodes exist but:

    root2.data != root3.data

then they cannot be mirrors.

    return false;

---

## Recursive Step

If both nodes exist and their values match, I recursively compare the opposite branches.

    boolean b = mirror(root2.left, root3.right);
    boolean c = mirror(root2.right, root3.left);

Both comparisons must be true.

    return b && c;

The important part is that recursion is not doing anything mysterious.

I am simply asking the same mirror question on smaller pairs of subtrees.

---

## Main Insight

The interesting part of this problem was not learning a new traversal.

It was changing the way I looked at the tree.

Initially:

    Symmetry
        |
        v
    Level Order
        |
        v
      Queue

But that started getting complicated.

Then I noticed:

    Symmetry
        |
        v
    Two Subtrees
        |
        v
    Compare Them as Mirrors
        |
        v
    Recursively Compare Opposite Branches

Once I saw the tree as two halves, the recursive solution became almost natural.

---

 Complexity

Every node is visited once.

Time

O(n)

Space

O(h) due to the recursion stack, where h is the height of the tree.

Balanced tree → O(log n)
Skewed tree → O(n)
 What I Learned

The main takeaway for me:

A recursive problem often becomes simple once I identify exactly what smaller version of the same question I need to ask.

Here, the smaller question was not:

"Is this subtree symmetric?"

It was:

"Are these two subtrees mirrors of each other?"

That small change in perspective turned what initially looked like a queue/traversal problem into a straightforward recursive comparison.